  "query_preview": "..\nToday and Sunday: The upper level pattern across the Pacific \nNorthwest remains under a cool and unsettled flow out of the \nsouthwest. Partly to mostly cloudy skies for some areas, while other \nareas in north-central Washington and northern Idaho have improved \nto mostly clear skies on this last day of October. The upper level \nLow pressure system that is digging into northern California is \nleaving the Inland Northwest in a cool and Fall-like weather pattern \nfor this weekend, keeping temperatures near or just above seasonal \nnormal for end of October/start of November (normal max/min temps \nfor Spokane are 49/34 and Lewiston 55/38, respectively). Pockets of \nfog and dense fog arrived early this morning for the West Plains, \nthe Palouse, and some areas of the Spokane metro. Clouds have since \ncleared for most areas and winds have shifted to more easterly. \nChances are low for a repeat of dense fog on Sunday given the \nincoming rain and moisture, but not a zero chance.\n\nMost of the energy associated with the Low pressure system has \nshifted farther south, so earlier predictions (days before) of rain \nshowers for Tonight continue to wane and become less. The latest \nhigh-res guidance (REFS and HRRR) keeps the precipitation chances \nfor the Inland Northwest confined to the Lewiston-Pullman/Palouse \narea beginning after 8 PM and closer to 10 PM. The near-surface \nenvironment is dry so seeing much in the way of measureable precip \nmore than a few hundredths is unlikely (10% chance of 0.01\" through \n11 PM). The better chances for and more prominent band of \nprecipitation will arrive between 11 PM and 11 AM. A 40-60% chance \nof 0.05 - 0.08 rain is expected from Lewiston north to Cusick, WA \nand east along the I-95 corridor from Lewiston to Hayden, Idaho. \n\nAn influence of cooler and drier air from the west (precipitable \nwater climo values at 100-120% of normal will transition to 70-80% \nof normal) should keep the chances for precipitation to a narrow \nperiod of time this weekend. The energy from the Low will shift \neastward through the day Sunday leaving the start of the week with \nmild and dry weather. \n\nMonday through Friday: High pressure behind the departing system \nwill build into the Northwest resulting in seasonal temperatures \n(near normal for early November) and dry conditions through  mid \nweek. One notable change I've seen while looking at the forecast is \nthe introduction of a few hours Monday morning (5 - 8 AM) of the \npotential for freezing rain across the upper Columbia Basin (Hwy 2 \ncorridor) and late Monday night overnight into early Tuesday morning \nthe chances of snow showers in the Cascade mountains. The freezing \nrain potential noted by the National Blend of Models is a small \nperiod of time and about 20% chance right now. The environment \ndoesn't seem as favorable with dry air and cool but not overly cold \ntemperatures aloft or near the surface. Needless to say, my \nconfidence is not great but I'd be doing a dis-service if I didn't \nmention the potential. Snow amounts in the Cascades above 3500/4000 \nfeet look favorable for several inches of accumulation for Wednesday \nthrough Friday (35-60% chance by end of Friday for amounts above \n4000 feet). /Dewey",
